  "text": "**Solutions 1 and 2 are ruled out by an ordering argument.** The curves are non-intersecting with $\\mathcal{H}^{(t)}_{n+1}(x) < \\mathcal{H}^{(t)}_n(x)$ almost surely, so $e^{\\mathcal{H}^{(t)}_{n+1}(x) - \\mathcal{H}^{(t)}_n(x)}$ lies strictly in $(0,1)$ and its expectation is strictly less than 1.\n\n- **Solution 2** gives 1, which is impossible.\n- **Solution 1** gives $1/t$, which exceeds 1 for $0 < t < 1$, so it is impossible there too. Its derivation (a $t$-derivative identity) is also unsupported.\n\n**Solution 3** gives $1/2$, which lies in $(0,1)$ and is independent of $n$, $x$ and $t$, as the question implies. Its derivation is heuristic: the claimed \"Ward-type identity\" and \"energy sum rule\" are asserted rather than proved. A rough check is consistent with it. If the gap $G = \\mathcal{H}_n - \\mathcal{H}_{n+1}$ were Gamma(1)-distributed, then $\\mathbb{E}[e^{-G}] = 1/2$. I could not verify the exact value from the Gibbs property alone, so my confidence in $1/2$ is low. It is the only candidate that passes the basic constraints, so I select it.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
